    {
      "id": "qtm-m2-quartiles-iqr-c2",
      "heading": "Interquartile range: formula, purpose and example",
      "explanation_md": "**IQR = Q3 - Q1.** It is the **range of the middle 50% of the data**.\n\n**Purpose (from the slide):**\n- Measures the **spread of the middle 50%** of the data.\n- Useful for **identifying outliers** and understanding data variability.\n- **Less affected by extreme values than the range**: it overcomes the sensitivity to extreme data values.\n\n**Limitation:** it **ignores information outside the middle 50%**, so it can overlook important dispersion in the tails.\n\n**Slide example (70 values):** Q3 = 525, Q1 = 445, so **IQR = 525 - 445 = 80**.\n\n**Raw (ungrouped) data positions:**\n- Q1 = the ((n + 1)/4)th term\n- Q2 = the ((n + 1)/2)th term (median)\n- Q3 = the (3(n + 1)/4)th term",
      "example_ids": [
        "qtm-ex-iqr-70"
      ],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Interquartile range (IQR)",
          "meaning": "Q3 - Q1; the range for the middle 50% of the data.",
          "source_ref": "qtm--module-2#30"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "IQR = Q3 - Q1; spread of the middle 50%; less affected by extremes than the range but ignores the tails. Example: 525 - 445 = 80.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
