    {
      "id": "qtm-m2-quartiles-iqr-q-f0cf21c0",
      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Explain quartiles and the interquartile range, show how quartiles are found for raw and grouped data, and explain why the IQR is better than the range.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Introduction.** Quartiles divide ordered data into four groups; the IQR uses them to describe spread.\n\n**Quartiles.** Q1 = 25th percentile, Q2 = 50th percentile = median, Q3 = 75th percentile. The slide's line from Min to Max shows 25% below Q1, 50% below the median and 75% below Q3.\n\n**Raw data.** Q1 is the ((n + 1)/4)th term, Q2 the ((n + 1)/2)th term, Q3 the (3(n + 1)/4)th term.\n\n**Grouped data.** Qk = L + ((k × n/4 - F)/f) × h, where L is the lower boundary of the quartile class, F the cumulative frequency before it, f its frequency and h the class width.\n\n**IQR.** IQR = Q3 - Q1, the spread of the middle 50%. Example: 525 - 445 = 80 against a range of 190.\n\n**Why better.** It is less affected by extreme values and helps identify outliers. Its weakness: it ignores information outside the middle 50%.\n\n**Conclusion.** Use the IQR when extremes would distort the range.",
